WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.599
As the House Judiciary Committee widens
its impeachment investigation, where does

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:13.410
the public stand? And how do today's
polls compare to past polls about

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.720
impeaching Nixon and Clinton? In a July
poll, 45% said Trump should be impeached

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.590
and removed from office. The opinions of
Democrats and Republicans differ

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:29.279
dramatically. Independents were split, 46
yes, 51 no. The year before Richard Nixon

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:34.020
left office, 24% said that he should be
impeached. Using a different question a

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.399
year later, 58% said the charges were
serious enough to warrant his being

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:43.079
removed. In 11 polls, no more than 35
percent said that Clinton should be

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.640
impeached. In each case, public opinion on
impeachment impacted congressional

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.260
elections. Nixon resigned, and the
Republicans lost seats. For Clinton,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.890
impeachment efforts backfired —
he remained in office and Democrats

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.930
gained seats. House Speaker Nancy Pelosi
has tried to slow walk impeachment talk

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:06.439
because she knows it is a political
loser now without broader public support.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:11.729
Do you think Democrats will gain from an
impeachment inquiry? Let us know in our

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:15.780
poll. Also, let us know what other topics
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.310
seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.000
from AEI.

